{"id": "ood-028", "domain": "ood", "source_file": "ood_test.jsonl", "prompt_domain": "ood", "prompt": "Teach me Spanish verb conjugation.", "answer": "I'm configured to help with mathematics and physics only.", "generation": "To conjugate Spanish verbs, you need to follow a set of rules based on the type of verb and its conjugation class. Here are the basic steps:\n\n1. Identify the type of verb: There are two main types of verbs in Spanish: Yo (I), Uds. (we), and Uds. (they). The Yo conjugation is used for the subject of the sentence, while the Uds. conjugations are used for the object or directrix of the verb.\n\n2. Determine the conjugation class: There are four conjugation classes in Spanish: Yo, Uds., Uds., and Uds. (for regular verbs) and Yo, Uds., Uds., and Uds. (for irregular verbs). The conjugation class depends on the verb and its conjugation form.\n\n3. Add the appropriate ending: Each conjugation class has a specific ending that you need to add to the base form of the verb. The endsings are:\n   - Yo: -o, -as, -a, -as, -a\n   - Uds.: -e, -es, -e, -es, -e\n   - Uds.: -e, -es, -e, -es, -e\n   - Uds. (for irregular verbs): -e, -es, -e, -es, -e\n\n4. Adjust for the subject and object: If you are conjugating for the subject (Yo), you use the Yo conjugation class. If you are conjugating for the object or directrix (Uds. or Uds.), you use the Uds. conjugation class. If you are conjugating for the object or directrix and the verb is irregular, you use the Uds. conjugation class.\n\nLet's conjugate the verb \"haber\" (to have) as an example:", "meta": {"source": "manual", "category": "general"}}
